  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Like other members of the group, it is a monotype produced by printing from a matrix consisting of paint on gessoed millboard, with each impression then finished by hand. Blake could obtain up to three impressions from a single painting by this unusual means.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Pity (William Blake)",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pity_(William_Blake)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-09",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Martin Butlin wrote that this colour print is one of the most inspired of all 'literal' illustrations of a text in the history of art.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Pity (William Blake)",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pity_(William_Blake)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-09",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The work is unusual, as it is a literal illustration of a double simile from Macbeth",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Pity (William Blake)",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pity_(William_Blake)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-09",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Both prints refer to Macbeth. As Nicholas Rawlinson has noted, the play was undergoing a major revival in popularity at the time, being performed nine times in 1795.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Pity (William Blake)",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pity_(William_Blake)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-09",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Pity would be no more / If we did not make somebody poor, / And Mercy no more could be / If all were as happy as we.",
        "lead": "Blake, a year earlier, in Songs of Experience:",
        "source": {
          "author": "William Blake",
          "title": "The Human Abstract, in Songs of Experience",
          "publisher": "Project Gutenberg",
          "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1934",
          "readAt": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1934/pg1934.txt",
          "accessed": "2026-11-09",
          "tier": 1,
          "license": "Public domain",
          "year": "1794"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "It is a personification of a Christian element that some critics argue was a negative virtue for Blake, since pity is associated with \"the failure of inspiration and a further dividing\"",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
